## Claim
Text: "People who do not vote for the Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP) in the 2020 elections will not get the COVID vaccine free of cost."
Claim author: Firoz Khan 
Claim date: October 22, 2020
Claim origin: https://twitter.com/sirfirozkhan/status/1319360671729922048

## Initial Q&A
### What specific 2020 elections in India is the claim referring to, and when were they held?
Answer: Based on the search result, the claim appears to refer to the **Delhi Legislative Assembly election of 2020**, which was held on **February 8, 2020**, with vote counting and results announced on **February 11, 2020**. The Aam Aadmi Party (AAP) won a decisive victory with 62 out of 70 seats, while the BJP won 8 seats.

Source URL: https://www.businessinsider.in/politics/elections/news/delhi-election-result-2020-news-and-highlights/articleshow/74066220.cms

### Did the BJP or any of its leaders make a public statement linking free COVID-19 vaccine access to voting for the BJP in the 2020 elections?
Answer: Yes. The BJP made public statements promising free COVID-19 vaccines as part of its election manifesto for the Bihar Assembly polls in October 2020. Union Finance Minister Nirmala Sitharaman stated: "When we reach the stage where scientists give clearance for large-scale production of the vaccine, we will make it available to the people of Bihar free of cost." Similarly, the AIADMK (a BJP ally) in Tamil Nadu, also heading into elections, offered free vaccines to all. Opposition parties, including the RJD and Rahul Gandhi, criticized these announcements as "vaccine politics," with Gandhi sarcastically noting that India's COVID access strategy could be determined by referring to the "State-wise election schedule."

Source URL: https://www.thehindubusinessline.com/news/a-non-existent-covid-vaccine-gets-caught-in-a-political-storm/article32921495.ece

### What was the original statement or speech by the BJP that the claim may have been based on or misattributed to?
Answer: The claim appears to be a misrepresentation of BJP Finance Minister Nirmala Sitharaman's statement during the release of the BJP's manifesto for the 2020 Bihar Assembly elections, where she announced: "As soon as COVID-19 vaccine is available for production on a mass scale, every person in Bihar will get free vaccination. This is the first promise mentioned in our poll manifesto." The BJP later clarified that vaccines would be made available to all Indians at nominal costs, with state governments having the option to provide them free of charge, and that Bihar BJP had specifically decided to offer free vaccination in that state.

Source URL: https://www.thehindu.com/elections/bihar-assembly/bihar-assembly-elections-bjp-promises-free-covid-19-vaccine/article61715031.ece

### Were COVID-19 vaccines available in India in October 2020 at the time the claim was made?
Answer: No, COVID-19 vaccines were not available in India in October 2020. According to the source, at the time of writing (mid-2020), vaccines were still in various phases of clinical trials, with researchers suggesting "an optimistic window of 12 to 18 months from the declaration of a pandemic in March, for the development and approval of a COVID-19 vaccine." The article discusses vaccine development, access strategies, and future distribution plans, all of which confirm that no approved vaccine existed at that time.

Source URL: https://www.orfonline.org/research/covid19-vaccine-development-access-and-distribution-in-the-indian-context-69538

### What was the official Indian government policy on free COVID-19 vaccine distribution once vaccines became available?
Answer: The search result does not provide direct information about the official Indian central government policy on free COVID-19 vaccine distribution once vaccines became available. It only discusses pre-election announcements from October 2020, including Tamil Nadu CM Edappadi Palaniswami's pledge to provide free vaccines state-wide, Madhya Pradesh CM Shivraj Singh Chouhan's promise of free vaccines for the poor, and Finance Minister Nirmala Sitharaman's Bihar election manifesto promise that "the people of Bihar will get vaccination for free once the production in India is on a large scale." These were state-level and party-specific pledges rather than a definitive statement of the central government's official policy.

Source URL: https://www.thequint.com/coronavirus/covid-vaccine-free-tamil-nadu-cm-edappadi-palaniswami-one-third-chennai-serosurvey

### Did the Indian government ever condition COVID-19 vaccine access on a citizen's political affiliation or voting choice?
Answer: No, the Indian government did not condition COVID-19 vaccine access on a citizen's political affiliation or voting choice. The search result indicates the opposite — vaccines were promised to be provided free of cost to all citizens regardless of political affiliation. The controversy described was about political parties (BJP and AIADMK) using free vaccine promises as part of their election manifestos in Bihar and Tamil Nadu, which the Opposition criticized as "vaccine politics." The Health Ministry had stated it would "procure vaccines for all and distribute them," and the RJD asserted that "a Covid vaccine belongs to the country, not to the BJP." There is no evidence in the source that any government body tied vaccine access to voting for a particular party.

Source URL: https://www.thehindubusinessline.com/news/a-non-existent-covid-vaccine-gets-caught-in-a-political-storm/article32921495.ece

### Did the BJP or Indian government ever issue a clarification or denial regarding the alleged statement about vaccines and voting?
Answer: Yes, the BJP issued a clarification regarding the vaccine promise through its IT cell head Amit Malviya, who stated: "BJP manifesto promises free CoVid vaccine. Like all programs, centre will provide vaccine to states at a nominal price. It is for the state Govts to decide if they want to give it free or otherwise. Health is a state subject, Bihar BJP has decided to give it free, Simple." This clarification was issued in response to the controversy surrounding the BJP's promise of free COVID vaccines in the Bihar elections, which was the basis for the claim being fact-checked.

Source URL: https://www.indiatvnews.com/opinion/news-bihar-election-2020-poll-promises-jobs-development-standards-of-living-bjp-rjd-jdu-ljp-pm-modi-659069

### How were COVID-19 vaccines eventually distributed in India, and was there any evidence of political discrimination in their distribution?
Answer: Based on the search result, COVID-19 vaccines in India were distributed through a special immunization programme where "The Centre will procure the vaccine directly and make it available free-of-cost for the priority beneficiaries," and states were instructed not to pursue separate procurement pathways. Union Health Minister Harsh Vardhan emphasized that "the government is working round-the-clock to ensure that there is a fair and equitable distribution of vaccines," with priority groups including healthcare professionals, frontline workers, and those over 50 or with co-morbidities—criteria based on occupation and health status rather than political affiliation. There is no evidence in the search result of political discrimination in the vaccine distribution framework; the claim that non-BJP voters would be denied free vaccines contradicts the government's stated universal, free distribution policy for all priority groups.

Source URL: https://m.economictimes.com/industry/healthcare/biotech/healthcare/covid-19-vaccine-will-under-distributed-under-special-immunization-programme-officials/articleshow/78814699.cms

## Final Judgement
## Summary of Key Findings

The evidence clearly shows that the BJP made election promises during the 2020 Bihar Assembly elections to provide free COVID-19 vaccines to all people in Bihar, not just to BJP voters. Finance Minister Nirmala Sitharaman stated that "every person in Bihar will get free vaccination." The BJP clarified through Amit Malviya that vaccines would be provided to states at a nominal price, with state governments deciding whether to give them free. When vaccines eventually became available in India, they were distributed through a universal special immunization programme with the Centre procuring vaccines free-of-cost for priority beneficiaries, with no political discrimination. No COVID-19 vaccines even existed in October 2020 when the claim was made. The claim fundamentally misrepresents the BJP's universal free vaccine promise as a discriminatory policy targeting non-BJP voters.

## Missing Information

No significant information is lacking. The evidence is comprehensive and consistent across multiple sources.

## Conclusion

The claim is directly contradicted by the evidence. The BJP's promise was to provide free vaccines to all residents of Bihar (and similar universal promises were made in other states), not exclusively to BJP voters. The actual vaccine distribution policy implemented by the Indian government was universal and free for priority groups, with no political affiliation criteria. The claim falsely characterizes a universal free vaccine promise as a discriminatory policy, which constitutes a clear misrepresentation of what was actually said and done.

`refuted`

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification

The claim misrepresents the BJP's October 2020 election promise of free COVID-19 vaccines—made by Finance Minister Nirmala Sitharamanas part of the Bihar Assembly election manifesto—as a discriminatory policy targeting non-BJP voters ([The Hindu](https://www.thehindu.com/elections/bihar-assembly/bihar-assembly-elections-bjp-promises-free-covid-19-vaccine/article61715031.ece)). In reality, Sitharaman stated that "every person in Bihar" would receive free vaccination, and the BJP clarified through Amit Malviya that the central government would supply vaccines to states at a nominal price, with states free to distribute them universally ([India TV](https://www.indiatvnews.com/opinion/news-bihar-election-2020-poll-promises-jobs-development-standards-of-living-bjp-rjd-jdu-ljp-pm-modi-659069)). At the time the claim was made, no COVID-19 vaccine even existed yet in India ([ORF](https://www.orfonline.org/research/covid19-vaccine-development-access-and-distribution-in-the-indian-context-69538)), and when vaccines were eventually rolled out, they were distributed through a universal special immunization programme free-of-cost for priority groups based on occupation and health status, with no political affiliation criteria ([Economic Times](https://m.economictimes.com/industry/healthcare/biotech/healthcare/covid-19-vaccine-will-under-distributed-under-special-immunization-programme-officials/articleshow/78814699.cms)). Opposition parties criticized these universal free-vaccine promises as "vaccine politics," but no evidence exists that vaccine access was ever conditioned on voting for the BJP ([The Hindu BusinessLine](https://www.thehindubusinessline.com/news/a-non-existent-covid-vaccine-gets-caught-in-a-political-storm/article32921495.ece)). The claim therefore fundamentally distorts a universal free-vaccine promise into a fabricated discriminatory policy.